Draw the caption buttons of the browser from QtCurve

The browser paints the caption buttons of its own frame. It takes each
glyph from the icon theme of GTK, and it colours the hover ground from
the title bar pair. That caption matches no other window of the desktop.

The sheet now holds a caption block. The block takes the appearance of
each button away, and it gives the icon of the button one inline image.
The glyphs are the family of the window decoration of QtCurve. Minimize
takes the bar, maximize takes the window symbol, restore takes that
symbol with a second window behind it, and close takes the cross. A
window without focus takes a second set of images, from the inactive
foreground that Button::glyphColor reads.

firefox/writer/caption.cpp takes every measure from the decoration.
Button::paint gives the square 11 pixels at the 24 pixel button of the
browser. Button::drawGlyph gives the stroke 2 pixels, the frame 1 pixel,
the bar 2 pixels and the restore step 2.75 pixels. The block carries the
2 pixel gap and the 4 pixel edge pad of the decoration. The button size
stays the 24 pixels of the browser, with its hit target.

The hover moves the colour of the symbol and nothing else. No rule of
the hover carries a ground, a ring or a pill. The rule at rest takes the
ground away in every state. The line art fades at rest under the hover
symbol setting, and the alpha byte of the image carries that fade. The
close button takes the close colour of the decoration under the pointer.

assert-ff-caption.sh decodes each image of a sheet of the build. It
reads the shape, the measures and the colour of every image, and the
property lines of every hover rule. Ten doctored copies must fail it.

The change sits on the branch work/firefox-theme of the local tree, as
commit 6 of 14. The branch holds those fourteen commits above the head of
work/gtk4-theme, which merge request !15 on system/qtcurve holds. The fork
wyn/qtcurve holds work/firefox-theme. Merge request !29 on system/qtcurve
holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

